# HTML Pro Rules

Read this file before writing any HTML or Sass. Enforces clean, accessible, responsive output.

## Sass / CSS Rules

- Do **NOT** use variables, mixins, functions, loops, or partials.
- Do **NOT** write global element styles like `h2 { }` or `p { }`.
- Do **NOT** add styling classes to heading tags (`h1`–`h6`). Headings must inherit styling from their parent wrapper.

**Correct:** `.hero-section h2 { }`  
**Wrong:** `.section-title { }`, `h2 { }`

## HTML Rules

- Do **NOT** add classes to heading (`h1`–`h6`), `p`, or `ul li` tags. Style them via parent selectors: `.hero-section h2 { }`
- Use Bootstrap 4 grid when layout columns are required.

## Responsive Rules

- Mobile-first implementation required.
- Fully responsive with no horizontal scroll at any breakpoint.
- Verify layout at: 320px, 768px, 992px, 1200px.
- Avoid fixed widths that break layout; images must scale properly.

## Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA)

- All interactive elements keyboard accessible with logical tab order.
- All images have descriptive `alt` text; decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Use `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` for toggles.
- Do **NOT** duplicate landmark roles; do **NOT** assign `role="main"` if `<main>` exists.


## Project Integration Rules

- Check `base.scss` for typography and heading styles before adding new styles.
- Check `variable-mixins-breakpoints.scss` for existing variables, mixins, and breakpoints.
- Reuse existing styles whenever possible.

- If a heading or paragraph style already exists, do NOT redefine it.
- Always use existing variables and mixins when available.

- Only add new styles in `main.scss` if no existing style matches the requirement.

- Keep HTML minimal, semantic, and free of unnecessary wrappers.